    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1085">

                <pb facs="1438" n="1428" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg1428"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">033.1193 Nelson, Donald M./5–3045: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-05-30T13:00:00+07:00">May 30, 1945—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 30—9:43 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>876. Work of Nelson Mission was discussed at length last night at a meeting
                    attended by Kearney<anchor n="13" xml:id="d1085-f1"/> and Brooks,<anchor n="14"
                        xml:id="d1085-f2"/> General Olmsted,<anchor n="15" xml:id="d1085-f3"/>
                    representatives of NEA [<hi rendition="#italic">FEA</hi>]<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d1085-f4"/> and of the Embassy including Robertson<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d1085-f5"/> just arrived. It was agreed among us that, assuming it
                    may be possible to obtain a more effective degree of cooperation from the
                    Chinese authorities (which will require early discussion with and positive
                    action by the Generalissimo), it will be recommended that the Mission be
                    retained in approximately its present form at least until a port and corridor
                    for the supply of China have been opened. Detailed report by air follows, please
                    inform Locke.<anchor n="18" xml:id="d1085-f6"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hurley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1085-f1" n="13" xml:id="d1085-f1-content">
                <p>A. T. Kearney, ranking officer of the American Production Mission to China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1085-f2" n="14" xml:id="d1085-f2-content">
                <p>E. P. Brooks, American Production Mission to China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1085-f3" n="15" xml:id="d1085-f3-content">
                <p>Brig. Gen. George H. Olmsted, G–5, Civil Affairs Officer, China Theater.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1085-f4" n="16" xml:id="d1085-f4-content">
                <p>Foreign Economic Administration (FEA).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1085-f5" n="17" xml:id="d1085-f5-content">
                <p>Walter S. Robertson, Minister Counselor for Economic Affairs in China.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1085-f6" n="18" xml:id="d1085-f6-content">
                <p>E. A. Locke, Jr., Assistant to Donald M. Nelson.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
